        "q2": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Our Hyde Park bungalow is in the historic district. Does that affect a railing?",
            "answer": "<p>It can. The City of Tampa's Architectural Review Commission reviews exterior changes in local historic districts such as Hyde Park; Ybor City has its own Barrio Latino Commission. A front porch railing visible from the street is more likely to need review than a back deck or a pool fence. Check with the city's historic preservation staff before ordering, and bring the product specifications and a finish sample.</p>",
            "pages": ""
          }
        },
        "q3": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Can the spigots mount on a paver pool deck?",
            "answer": "<p>The spigots are designed to fix to wood or concrete, not to loose pavers. On a paver deck, the anchors have to reach a concrete slab or a footing beneath the pavers, and the engineering documents show the blocking details. If your pool deck is pavers over sand with no slab, send photos and measurements before ordering and we will tell you what the spigots need. Screws and anchors are sold separately.</p>",
            "pages": ""
          }
        },
        "q4": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Brushed Stainless or Matte Black for a bay-front deck?",
            "answer": "<p>Both are the same 2205 marine-grade stainless underneath, with the same 10-year no-rust promise, so the choice is about looks. Brushed Stainless suits a Mediterranean or coastal-white house; Matte Black reads modern and pairs with bronze screen enclosures. On the water, rinse either finish with fresh water now and then. The <a href=\"/products/glass-hardware-sample-kit\">sample kit</a> puts both finishes and the glass in your hand before you decide.</p>",
